<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William C. Gorgas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Crawford Gorgas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William C. Gorgas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army physician who, as chief sanitary officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> from 1904, brought yellow fever and malaria under control by destroying the mosquitoes that carried them. Applying methods he had used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-reed">Walter Reed</a>'s work on yellow fever transmission, he made the American construction of the canal possible. He later served as Surgeon General of the United States Army from 1914 to 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-c-gorgas.jpg" alt="William C. Gorgas" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing, photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GORGAS,_W.C._GENERAL_LCCN2016857062_Trim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Crawford Gorgas was born on 3 October 1854 in Toulminville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, the first of six children of Josiah Gorgas and Amelia Gayle Gorgas; his maternal grandparents were Governor John Gayle and the diarist Sarah Ann Haynsworth Gayle. He studied at the University of the South and at Bellevue Hospital Medical College and joined the US Army Medical Corps in June 1880, serving at Fort Clark, Fort Duncan and Fort Brown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Fort Brown in 1882–84 he was sent to control an epidemic of yellow fever. He caught the disease while caring for Marie Cook Doughty, who nearly died of it; they recovered together and married, and their immunity meant they were sent to later outbreaks. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> he became Chief Sanitary Officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> in 1898, where with Robert Ernest Noble he drew on the work of Major Walter Reed and the Cuban doctor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-finlay">Carlos Finlay</a> on mosquito transmission. By draining the breeding ponds of the Aedes aegypti mosquito and isolating patients in screened rooms, he brought yellow fever cases down from 784 to zero within a year.</p><p>On the canal he drained ponds and swamps, fumigated, used mosquito netting and built public water systems. Finlay's approach was at first met with scepticism and opposition, but the measures saved thousands of lives among the workers. Gorgas was president of the American Medical Association in 1909–10, and in 1914 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington-goethals">George Washington Goethals</a> received the first Public Welfare Medal of the National Academy of Sciences. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he asked for the founding of the Boston School of Occupational Therapy in 1918. He was also a Georgist, who held that Henry George's Single Tax would bring sanitary living conditions to the poor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gorgas retired in 1918 at the age of 64. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> made him an honorary KCMG at the Queen Alexandra Military Hospital in the United Kingdom, where he died on 3 July 1920; he was given a funeral in St Paul's Cathedral and is buried at Arlington National Cemetery. The Gorgas Memorial Institute of Tropical and Preventive Medicine was founded in 1921, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>'s Ancon Hospital was renamed after him in 1928, and his name stands on the frieze of the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William C. Gorgas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States built the canal between 1904 and 1914, after <b>William Gorgas</b>’s sanitation campaign brought yellow fever and malaria under control, employing tens of thousands of workers, many of them from Barbados, Jamaica and other Caribbean islands, whose descendants form an important Afro-Caribbean, English-speaking community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>American construction began in 1904; <b>William Gorgas</b>'s campaign against mosquitoes conquered yellow fever, and George Washington Goethals completed the canal, which opened in August 1914.</blockquote><a href="/state/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mobile, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.73,-88.05&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Mobile on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2270 1043 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William C. Gorgas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2720 1313h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2720 1313h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.73,-88.05&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
